The tool allows to create, delete and switch between virtual ter‐
minals  on  display  devices  controlled  by  the wscons terminal
framework if the underlying display hardware driver supports mul‐
tiple screens.  Further it controls the assignment  of  keyboards
to displays.  The argument specifies which virtual terminal is to
be  configured;  the allowed numbers are from 0 to an implementa‐
tion‐specified value (currently 7, allowing for 8 virtual  termi‐
nals on a display).  In keyboard configuration mode, it specifies
the  device  to  attach  or detach.  Without further option argu‐
ments, a virtual terminal is created with implementation specific
properties and a default terminal emulation variant  selected  at
kernel  compile time.  The options are: Delete the specified ter‐
minal.  A terminal opened by a program will not be deleted unless
the option is applied.  Terminals used by  the  operating  system
console or a graphics program (X server) cannot be deleted.  With
the flag, the keyboard specified by will be detached from the ws‐
cons  display.   With the flag, the multiplexor specified by will
be detached from the wscons display.  Specify the terminal emula‐
tion to use for the virtual terminal.  The set of available  ter‐
minal  emulations  is determined at kernel compile time.  See for
details.  Force deleting of a terminal even if it is in use by  a
user  space  program.   Specify  the control device of the wscons
display to operate on.  Default is Print the index of the current
virtual terminal.  Do keyboard related operations instead of vir‐
tual screen configuration.  Without other flags, a keyboard  will
be  attached to the display device.  The argument can be omitted,
in this case the first free keyboard will  be  used.   Do  multi‐
plexor  related  operations  instead of virtual screen configura‐
tion.  Without other flags, a multiplexor will be attached to the
display device.  Switch to the specified virtual terminal.  Spec‐
ify a screen type to use.  Screen types refer to display  format,
colour depth and other low‐level display properties.  Valid argu‐
ments are defined by the underlying display device driver.  Typi‐
cally,  the  utility  will  be  invoked  in system startup by the
script, controlled by the configuration file.  Configure screen 1
(i.e., the second), it will get the type and use the VT100 termi‐
nal emulation.  (Note: is a screen type offered  by  the  display
driver.   In this particular case, an 8×8‐font must be loaded be‐
fore to make the screen useful.  See  Connect  the  first  uncon‐
nected  keyboard to the display.  Create screen 3.  Delete screen
3.  Switch to screen 2.  There should be an easy  way  to  get  a
list  of the screen types available on a display, and of the emu‐
